 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Fixing isn't clearly the better choice right now. The 3-year fixed is more expensive than the discounted variable rate, so you'd be paying a premium for certainty. Whether that premium is worth it depends on your budget and loan features.\n\n**What banks are advertising (owner-occupier, September 2026, the latest RBA data):**\n\n| Product | Rate (% p.a.) |\n|---|---|\n| 3-year fixed | **7.02%** |\n| Variable, discounted | 6.80% |\n| Variable, standard | 8.77% |\n\n- **3-year fixed has risen about 1 percentage point** since its low of 5.52% in October 2025. It has climbed through 2026.\n- **The RBA cash rate target was 4.36%** (September 2026 average), up from 3.60% at the start of 2026. Fixed rates are reacting to this and to expectations of where rates are heading.\n- **Inflation is running above the target.** Trimmed mean (the RBA's preferred measure) was 3.6% and headline CPI was 4.0% in August 2026. That's why lenders are pricing in higher rates.\n\n**What this means for you:**\n- Fixing at 7.02% instead of the discounted variable at 6.80% costs about $15 a month more per $100,000 borrowed. On a $600,000 loan over 30 years, that's roughly $88 a month more.\n- You're paying for protection against further rate rises. If the RBA hikes again, the fixed rate protects you. If it holds or cuts, you'd have overpaid.\n- Fixing usually means losing offset account benefits (or paying for them), limits on extra repayments, and possible break costs if you sell or refinance.\n- If you're on a standard variable rate of 8.77%, switching to a discounted variable (6.80%) is the cheapest move available. That's a bigger saving than fixing.\n\n**Questions to ask yourself:**\n1. Could you handle your repayment if rates rose another 1 percentage point? If not, fixing (or at least a partial fix) is sensible.\n2. Do you rely on an offset account or make extra repayments? If so, floating is likely better.\n3. Are you planning to sell or refinance within 3 years?\n\nAdvertised rates aren't what most borrowers pay. Negotiated discounts can be large, so ask your lender or a broker for the rate you'd actually get. A split loan (part fixed, part variable) is a common middle option. Since this is a large decision, it's worth speaking to a mortgage broker or financial adviser who can see your full loan details.",
